# THE STEP CLOCK (0.17.48, the audit): a Jam key goes on the nearest sixteenth by the engine's own place in it (en_step_frac). Before, it was rounded
# by the time the UI's frame saw the sixteenth begin, read after the keys: a key just after the beat was often put on the next sixteenth. And a tap
# wholly in a sixteenth's back half wrapped its length to 255 (a hold to the bar's end); now both ends are rounded alike.
seed 0
wait 3000
turn SELECT 36 100
expect view 36
spin K1 3
wait 100
expect jamv 3
# just after a sixteenth begins: on that one (three times)
waitfor stepphase <9 2000
key 0 down
wait 1
expect capdelta 0
key 0 up
wait 300
waitfor stepphase <9 2000
key 2 down
wait 1
expect capdelta 0
key 2 up
wait 300
waitfor stepphase <9 2000
key 4 down
wait 1
expect capdelta 0
key 4 up
wait 300
# past its middle: on the next
waitfor stepphase >62 2000
key 5 down
wait 1
expect capdelta 1
# released before that next one comes (the same sixteenth still playing): one sixteenth long, not 255
waitfor stepphase >85 2000
key 5 up
wait 1
expect caplen 1
wait 300
# held about four sixteenths: four
waitfor stepphase <9 2000
key 7 down
wait 1
expect capdelta 0
waitfor stepphase >40 2000
wait 375
key 7 up
wait 1
expect caplen 4
